## SECTION 1: PROJECT OVERVIEW

### What is LineageLens?

LineageLens is an AI provenance tracking system for software teams. It answers one question that no other tool answers: **"Where did this code come from, and why?"**

AI coding assistants — Claude Code, Cursor, Aider, GitHub Copilot, Codeium, Continue, Sourcegraph Cody, Amazon Q, Gemini CLI, OpenAI Codex — now write large fractions of production codebases. The origin and intent of that code is invisible. LineageLens makes it visible. It captures every significant AI-assisted code insertion, links it to the prompt and model that produced it, stores it with structured metadata, and gives developers tools to search, explain, audit, and govern that code over time.

LineageLens works with any AI tool from any environment. The universal proxy (port 8788) intercepts traffic between your AI tools and their providers regardless of which editor or terminal you are using. The MCP server lets Claude Code CLI, Cursor, Continue, and any MCP-capable client query provenance without an IDE at all. GitHub Actions integrations run in CI/CD pipelines with no local editor involved. The VS Code extension is the primary automatic-capture host today — it gives the richest per-insertion metadata — but the product's core value (provenance records, semantic search, governance dashboard, risk scoring, usage reports) is entirely backend-driven and editor-agnostic. The backend is a self-hosted FastAPI service backed by PostgreSQL and optionally Neo4j.

An MCP server ships alongside the extension, letting any MCP-capable client (Claude Code, Cursor, Continue, etc.) query provenance records directly inside the AI chat without leaving the conversation.

### The Problem

AI coding tools are writing large portions of production software. This creates three compounding problems:

**Auditability.** When a bug appears in AI-generated code, there is no record of what prompt produced it, which model generated it, or what context surrounded the decision to insert it. Root-cause analysis guesses.

**Governance.** Security and compliance teams cannot answer basic questions: How much of our codebase was AI-generated? Which files have the highest AI insertion density? Which sessions produced high-risk code — security patterns, auth logic, eval calls?

**Lineage.** Code evolves. When AI-generated code is refactored, extended, or deleted, the relationship between generations is lost. There is no graph of how AI-contributed code has changed over time.

Traditional tools — debuggers, linters, code review bots — operate on what the code does, not where it came from. LineageLens is the missing layer that tracks origin, not behavior.

### Why This Matters

The risks are concrete. A prompt that was slightly wrong, a model that hallucinated an API, a session that wrote auth logic without full context — these are real failure modes that cause real incidents. Without provenance, root-cause analysis guesses. With it, you trace from the bug back to the exact AI session that wrote the code.

For regulated industries — fintech, healthcare, defense — AI code governance is becoming a compliance requirement. LineageLens gives teams the audit trail they need before regulators require it.

### Target Users

**Base users.** Individual engineers using AI tools heavily who want a personal record of what AI wrote in their codebase. They install the LineageLens Base VS Code extension: file-watcher capture, local JSON storage, no backend, no account, fully offline.

**Lite teams (1-10 engineers).** Small teams and indie developers who want a shared dashboard without Postgres complexity or per-seat pricing. They run LineageLens Lite: a single Docker container with SQLite, a browser-based setup wizard, and full proxy capture — free, runs on a $5 VPS.

**Plus workspaces (5-50 engineers).** Teams shipping AI-assisted code who need shared provenance records, semantic search across all insertions, governance dashboards, and AI contribution pattern review. They run LineageLens Plus with a PostgreSQL backend.

**Max organizations.** Large teams with compliance requirements, multi-workspace setups, full graph lineage tracking, and audit export needs. They run LineageLens Max with Neo4j-backed lineage graphs.

