### VS Code Extension (primary capture host)

The VS Code extension is one capture surface among several. It produces the richest per-insertion metadata automatically — file watcher, correlation engine, agent adapter registry, sidebar UI. But the product's value layer (provenance storage, semantic search, governance dashboard, risk scoring, MCP access, GitHub Actions) runs entirely in the backend and is independent of any editor. The architecture supports future JetBrains, Neovim, and CLI hosts through the provider-agnostic event schema, lightweight record builder, and adapter registry.

Key components:

- **`lineagelens-src/extension.ts`** — Main activation, file change listeners, insertion detection, provenance record assembly.
- **`lineagelens-src/proxy.ts`** — Local HTTP/HTTPS proxy (port 8080) that captures outbound LLM API traffic before it leaves the machine.
- **`lineagelens-src/correlation.ts`** — Matches a qualifying code insertion to a recent proxy-captured request/response pair using timing, file context, Levenshtein similarity, and confidence scoring. Also correlates against Claude Code hook events.
- **`lineagelens-src/agentAdapters/`** — Pluggable adapters (11 total) that identify which tool produced the insertion. Each adapter declares capabilities and evidence.
- **`lineagelens-src/eventSchema.ts`** — Provider-agnostic normalized event schema (version `lineagelens.provenance-event.v1`). All adapters produce the same shape.
- **`lineagelens-src/lightweightRecord.ts`** — Pure helper (no VS Code imports) for building provenance records from CLI adapters, scripts, or other non-extension hosts.
- **`lineagelens-src/hookListener.ts`** — Watches `~/.lineagelens/hook-events.jsonl` for Claude Code tool-use events written by the hook capture script.
- **`lineagelens-src/storage/`** — Abstraction layer with two implementations: `LocalStorageService` (VS Code global state or workspace file) and `BackendStorageService` (sends records to the FastAPI backend over WebSocket or HTTP).

### Universal LLM Proxy

The universal proxy (`lineagelens-proxy/proxy.py`) runs on port 8788. It is a transparent HTTP/HTTPS proxy that sits between any AI coding tool and its upstream AI provider. When tools are configured to use `http://localhost:8788` as their API base URL, all LLM traffic routes through this proxy. The proxy:

- Forwards requests to the correct upstream provider (Anthropic, OpenAI, etc.)
- Extracts prompt and response bodies from the intercepted traffic
- Sends provenance capture payloads to the backend at port 8787
- Supports CONNECT tunneling for HTTPS traffic (recorded as `tunnel_only`)

Supported configuration:
```bash
# Claude Code / Anthropic SDK
export ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8788

# OpenAI SDK / any compatible tool
export OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8788
```

Also works with: Cursor, Aider, Continue, Codeium/Windsurf, GitHub Copilot (via HTTP proxy), and any other tool that accepts a configurable API base URL.

### Agent Adapter Registry

The agent adapter registry (`lineagelens-src/agentAdapters/registry.ts`) runs all 11 registered adapters against each provenance event and selects the highest-confidence match. Adapters run in priority order; lower `order` value means the adapter is tried first when confidence ties occur.

**All 11 registered adapters (priority order):**

| Order | Adapter | Tool | Session Kind | Key Signals |
|-------|---------|------|-------------|-------------|
| 10 | `cursor` | Cursor IDE | `agentic` | `x-cursor-*` headers, `cursor` user-agent, `composer` payload |
| 15 | `copilot` | GitHub Copilot | `assistant` | `api.githubcopilot.com`, `x-github-token`, `editor-version` |
| 20 | `claude-code` | Claude Code CLI | `cli` / `agentic` | `anthropic-version`, `x-app: cli`, `claude-code/` user-agent |
| 25 | `codeium` | Codeium / Windsurf | `agentic` | `codeium.com`, `x-codeium-api-key`, `windsurf` signals |
| 30 | `aider` | Aider | `agentic` | `aider` user-agent, `pair programming` payload |
| 35 | `continue` | Continue.dev | `agentic` | `x-continue-version`, `x-continue-unique-id` |
| 40 | `cody` | Sourcegraph Cody | `agentic` | `cody-gateway.sourcegraph.com`, `x-sourcegraph-client` |
| 45 | `amazon-q` | Amazon Q Developer | `assistant` | `amazonaws.com`, `x-amz-target`, `x-amz-request-id` |
| 50 | `gemini-cli` | Gemini CLI | `cli` | `generativelanguage.googleapis.com`, `x-goog-api-client` |
| 55 | `codex-cli` | OpenAI Codex CLI | `cli` | `openai-codex` user-agent, o-series models on `api.openai.com` |
| 1000 | `legacy-heuristic` | Unknown | `unknown` | Keyword matching, lowest confidence, always fires last |

Each adapter returns a `NormalizedAgentContext` with: `toolName`, `provider`, `sessionId`, `conversationId`, `runId`, `modelName`, `userAgent`, `operationType`, `confidence`, `evidence[]`, `sessionKind`, `host`, `sessionSignature`.

The `inferProvider` function in `lineagelens-src/agentAdapters/shared.ts` resolves provider from host and model name for all supported providers: Anthropic, OpenAI, GitHub, Google, AWS, Codeium, Sourcegraph, OpenRouter.

### Lightweight Adapter Boundary

`lineagelens-src/lightweightRecord.ts` provides a pure TypeScript helper for building provenance records outside the VS Code extension — from a CLI, script, manual reviewer flow, or another editor integration. It accepts a minimal payload and produces the same `ProviderAgnosticProvenanceEvent` contract the extension uses.

Minimum required fields: `eventId`, `timestampIso`, `filePath`, `fileUri`, `languageId`, `insertedText`.

Optional fields enable richer records: `fullPromptMessages`, `rawModelResponse`, `modelName`, `agentContext`, `contextSnapshot`, `gitBranch`, `insertedChunks`, `captureStatus`.

The helper automatically infers `promptStatus` from the presence of prompt/response data, synthesizes a `PromptCorrelationResult`, and sets `shimName: 'lightweight-adapter'` so the backend can identify the source.

Four operating profiles:
- **`base`**: Everything stays in VS Code storage or workspace file. No backend.
- **`lite`**: Backend stores provenance in SQLite (BACKEND_MODE=solo). No Postgres, no Neo4j.
- **`plus`**: Backend stores provenance in PostgreSQL; Neo4j and vector search are optional.
- **`max`**: Full graph lineage (Neo4j) and vector search enabled.

### FastAPI Backend

Python 3.11+ async FastAPI application. Handles:

- User authentication (JWT, workspace-scoped)
- Provenance record ingest and storage
- Embedding generation and vector search
- LLM-powered explanation of provenance records
- Team management (invite, list members)
- Governance insights dashboard
- Real-time WebSocket ingest
- Static HTML/JS dashboard served at `/dashboard`

Entry point: `lineagelens-backend/app/main.py`. Default port: `8787`.

### MCP Server

`lineagelens-mcp/lineagelens-mcp.py` is a Python MCP server that lets any MCP-capable client (Claude Code, Cursor, Continue, etc.) query LineageLens provenance data directly inside the AI chat.

The server uses `FastMCP` from the `mcp` package and communicates over stdio. It authenticates against the LineageLens backend using either a static JWT or auto-login with username/password. Token caching with automatic re-login on 401 prevents session expiry.

**Authentication:**
```bash
# Option A — pre-obtained JWT
export LINEAGELENS_ACCESS_TOKEN="<your-jwt>"

# Option B — username + password (auto-login)
export LINEAGELENS_USERNAME="your-username"
export LINEAGELENS_PASSWORD="your-password"
export LINEAGELENS_BACKEND_URL="http://localhost:8787"
```

**Claude Code integration** — add to `~/.claude/settings.json` or `.claude/settings.json`:
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "lineagelens": {
      "command": "python",
      "args": ["/absolute/path/to/lineagelens-mcp/lineagelens-mcp.py"],
      "env": {
        "LINEAGELENS_USERNAME": "your-username",
        "LINEAGELENS_PASSWORD": "your-password",
        "LINEAGELENS_BACKEND_URL": "http://localhost:8787"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

**Cursor integration** — Cursor → Settings → MCP → Add Server, command: `python /absolute/path/to/lineagelens-mcp/lineagelens-mcp.py`, set the same three env vars.

**Install:**
```bash
cd lineagelens-mcp
pip install -r lineagelens-mcp-requirements.txt
```

### PostgreSQL + pgvector

PostgreSQL is the primary data store. The `pgvector` extension adds a 256-dimensional vector column to `provenance_records`, enabling cosine-distance semantic search.

Two tables:
- `provenance_records` — one row per captured AI insertion
- `user_accounts` — one row per user, workspace-scoped with role and token versioning

SQLAlchemy async ORM with `asyncpg` driver. Migrations managed by Alembic.

### Neo4j (Max only)

Neo4j stores code lineage as a directed graph. On ingest, the backend creates:
- An `AIGeneratedBlock` node (logical block of AI-generated code)
- A `ProvenanceBlockVersion` node (one version of that block)
- A `HAS_VERSION` relationship linking block to version
- A `LATEST_VERSION` pointer

Disabled in Base and Plus (`NEO4J_ENABLED: false`). In Max mode it provides graph traversal for lineage evolution queries.

### AI Analysis Layer

**Embeddings.** The backend generates a 256-dimensional embedding per record. Provider is configurable:
- `hash` (Base default) — deterministic hash-based embedding, no API key required
- `openai` (Plus/Max) — `text-embedding-3-small`, enables semantic similarity search

**LLM Explain.** Calls an OpenAI-compatible chat completions endpoint to generate plain-English explanations. Model defaults to `gpt-4o-mini`. Falls back to template-based explanation when no API key is configured. The extension also supports local Ollama (e.g., `qwen2.5-coder:7b`) for fully offline explanations.

### Data Flow: End-to-End

```
1. Developer receives AI-generated code in VS Code
2. Extension file watcher detects the change
3. Insertion is extracted; net line count is computed (threshold: 4 lines)
4. Local extension proxy (8080) is checked for a matching request/response pair (30 s window)
   OR: Claude Code hook listener checks for matching hook events
   OR: Universal proxy (8788) has already captured the raw traffic
5. Agent adapter registry identifies the tool (11 adapters, best confidence wins)
6. ProvenanceRecord is assembled:
   - Inserted code, surrounding context, cursor position
   - Matched prompt/response (if captured) or hook event
   - Normalized event (provider-agnostic schema v1)
   - AST node-type snapshot (Tree-sitter)
   - Local embeddings
   - Risk metadata
7. Record is sent to backend over WebSocket (falls back to HTTP POST /ingest)
8. Backend normalizes and validates the payload (ingest_normalizer.py)
9. Backend generates 256-dim embedding vector (hash or OpenAI)
10. Record is written to PostgreSQL (provenance_records)
11. If Neo4j is enabled: AIGeneratedBlock + ProvenanceBlockVersion are created
12. Save lineage_node_id back to Postgres record
13. Record is indexed for vector search (IVFFLAT index)
14. Developer can search, view, explain, or review the record from the sidebar
15. MCP server tools surface the same records inside the AI chat on demand
```

